    "stem": "An 84-year-old man with Alzheimer disease is admitted for pneumonia. On the second night he becomes agitated, tries to climb out of bed, and insists he must go home, although he is calm and cooperative each morning. He has no pain, vital signs are normal, and a repeat exam is unchanged. Which of the following is the most appropriate initial management?",
    "options": [
      "Administer intravenous haloperidol",
      "Reduce nighttime noise and stimulation, reorient frequently, and keep the room lit by day and dim by night",
      "Administer oral lorazepam at bedtime",
      "Give diphenhydramine for sleep",
      "Apply soft wrist restraints"
    ],
    "answerIndex": 1,
    "explanation": "Evening worsening of confusion (sundowning) is treated first with nonpharmacologic measures: minimizing ambient noise and distractions, frequent reorientation, familiar objects or family presence, and a normal light-dark cycle.",
    "optionRationales": [
      "Antipsychotics are reserved for dangerous agitation refractory to nonpharmacologic measures and carry increased mortality in dementia.",
      "Correct: environmental and behavioral measures are first-line for sundowning; \"decrease ambient noise and distractions\" is the NBME-favored phrasing.",
      "Benzodiazepines worsen confusion, cause paradoxical agitation, and increase falls in older demented patients.",
      "First-generation antihistamines are strongly anticholinergic and precipitate or worsen delirium in the elderly.",
      "Restraints increase agitation, delirium, and injury and are a last resort only for imminent danger."
    ],
    "concept": "Behavioral disturbances in dementia, including sundowning, are managed nonpharmacologically first: reduce noise and clutter, reorient with clocks and calendars, maintain routine, ensure glasses and hearing aids, involve family, and preserve a day-night light cycle (not bright light all night). Avoid benzodiazepines and anticholinergics; use antipsychotics only briefly for severe, dangerous agitation."
